  "headline": "IISS report states Gulf states must rethink trillion-dollar defense following US-Iran war",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "A study by the International Institute for Strategic Studies (IISS) suggests that Gulf states may need to reconfigure their trillion-dollar defense systems following a war between the United States and Iran. The report indicates that the conflict has highlighted the questionable continued value of US bases and has shown that longstanding security partnerships with the US have failed to ensure adequate protection against Iranian aggression. According to the IISS, the war revealed that the acquisition of US weapons did not translate into US protection during a critical period and that regional militaries are not adequately prepared for the type of attritional air war they are facing. The report also notes that public opinion in the Gulf regarding the US has shifted from viewing the country as a security provider to a strategic liability. Additionally, the study suggests that US military bases may have provided Iran with a pretext for attacks against the Gulf states. The report identifies several shortcomings despite heavy investment in US armaments, including an overwhelming reliance on US-supplied air- and missile-defense systems, a failure to achieve regional defense integration, a shortage of minesweeping capability, limited retaliatory options, and prohibitive costs caused by using expensive, high-end systems to intercept relatively inexpensive drones and missiles."
    },
    {
      "text": "The IISS report states that in the weeks preceding an 8 April ceasefire, 12 regional states—predominantly in the Gulf—faced 4,670 Iranian one-way attack drones and more than 1,400 ballistic missiles. Nearly half of these attacks were aimed at the United Arab Emirates. While the report notes that the vast majority of these projectiles were repelled, it cites an estimate of damage to energy infrastructure as high as $58bn (£43bn). The study further notes that Iran would likely portray any reduction or complete withdrawal of US forces from the region as a strategic success and use it to embarrass Gulf states regarding their long-term pursuit of US protection. These findings follow comments from US Senator Chris Murphy, a Democrat and member of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, stating that a debate is occurring within the US military regarding the value of rebuilding damaged US bases in the Gulf."
    }
  ]
}

{"headline": "Netanyahu bloc leads in Israel election but fails to secure majority", "pages": 
[
  {
    "text": "Final results from Israel's legislative election show that neither Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu's bloc nor the opposition has won an outright majority in the 120-seat Knesset. Netanyahu's coalition of right-wing and far-right parties secured 53 seats, with his Likud party remaining the largest faction with 27 seats. The opposition camp, led by Gadi Eisenkot's centrist Yashar party, ended with 48 seats. Arab-led parties secured a record 19 seats, potentially holding the balance of power in the fragmented parliament. Both Netanyahu and Eisenkot have previously stated they will not work with these Arab-Israeli groups. Following the results, Israelis protested outside Eisenkot's home on October 29, 2026, urging opposition parties to join forces with Arab-Israeli groups to oust Netanyahu. Under Israeli law, President Isaac Herzog will consult with party leaders to assign a candidate 28 days to form a government, a period that can be extended by 14 days. If that fails, a second leader may be given 28 days, followed by a third candidate with 14 days. If all attempts fail, another election is called."
  },
  {
    "text": "On October 29, 2026, Netanyahu and the Likud party amplified allegations of election fraud, claiming Arab-Israeli parties had 'stolen' the polls through foreign funding. Specifically, the Likud party pushed claims that the Ra'am party received money from Qatar to influence voters. Ra'am leader Mansour Abbas denied any wrongdoing and stated he would welcome an investigation. The Joint List accused Netanyahu and his allies of conducting a 'campaign of incitement' due to the growing political influence of Arab voters. Government officials have so far provided no evidence to back the allegations. Eisenkot, via social media, described foreign interference as 'unacceptable and extremely serious' and called on the Shin Bet, Israel's domestic security service, to investigate the allegations fully and immediately without bias."
  }
]
}

{
  "headline": "Ghanaian President Mahama commends Vladimir Putin's willingness to resume Ukraine talks",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Addressing the Third Russia-Africa Summit in Moscow, Ghanaian President John Dramani Mahama commended Russian President Vladimir Putin for his willingness to resume trilateral talks regarding the conflict with Ukraine. Mahama expressed appreciation for Putin's call for a lasting peace agreement and security for both Russia and Ukraine, noting that global conflicts have caused deaths, suffering, and disruptions to global logistics and value chains. While calling for an end to global conflicts, Mahama also called for international cooperation against terrorism, specifically within the Alliance of Sahelian States. Reaffirming Ghana’s position of non-alignment, Mahama stated the country seeks friendships and partnerships that advance its development priorities."
    },
    {
      "text": "During the summit, Mahama invited Russian businesses to partner with Ghanaian firms across several sectors, including agro-processing, pharmaceutical, energy, transport infrastructure, the digital economy, and the production of fertiliser and agricultural machinery. He identified opportunities in cocoa, cashew, shea nuts, horticulture, mineral processing, and manufacturing, noting that Ghana's role as host of the African Continental Free Trade Area Secretariat makes it well-positioned to support investors across the continent. To grow trade between Africa and Russia, Mahama called for mechanisms for legitimate trade settlements, such as digital payment systems and local currencies. Regarding energy, Mahama identified Russia's comparative advantage in renewable and nuclear energy and disclosed that Ghana has initiated discussions with Rosatom to establish the country’s first nuclear power station. He recalled that in 1964, the Soviet Union provided Ghana with a two-megawatt research reactor and trained Ghanaian scientists in Russia to operate it."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Former British Columbia NDP ministers express frustration over snap election call",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Two former cabinet ministers in British Columbia's New Democratic Party (NDP) have criticized the decision to call a snap election that resulted in a significant loss for the party. According to a preliminary count, the NDP's seat count was reduced from 47 to 28, while the BC Conservatives secured a two-thirds majority of 63 seats. Outgoing forests minister Ravi Parmar stated the party and outgoing Premier David Eby made the wrong decision and that the party owes British Columbians an apology. Parmar described his level of frustration regarding the election call as a 10 out of 10. Former attorney general Niki Sharma noted that during an outgoing caucus meeting on October 28, 2026, Eby apologized and took full responsibility for calling the election. Sharma described the meeting as an emotional event as colleagues said farewell to departing MLAs. Both Sharma and Parmar have left open the possibility of seeking party leadership following Eby's resignation. Sharma stated she is not seeking the interim leadership position being discussed at an incoming caucus meeting on October 29, 2026, but remained non-committal regarding the permanent leadership."
    }
  ]
}

