{
  "headline": "Bangladesh and Thailand agree to expand trade and connectivity at 4th Foreign Office Consultations",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Bangladesh and Thailand have agreed to increase economic, trade, and diplomatic cooperation following the 4th Foreign Office Consultations (FOC) held at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Thailand in Bangkok. The Bangladesh delegation was led by Foreign Secretary Asad Alam Siam, while the Thai delegation was headed by Permanent Secretary Eksiri Pintaruchi, according to The Financial Express Bangladesh. Discussions addressed bilateral relations including trade, investment, maritime connectivity, labor migration, agriculture, energy, cultural exchanges, and climate change. To address the bilateral trade deficit, Bangladesh proposed duty reductions on its goods and encouraged Thailand to increase imports of jute products and pharmaceuticals. The Thai delegation agreed to evaluate these proposals and requested the early initiation of negotiations for a bilateral Free Trade Agreement (FTA). Regarding maritime connectivity, both nations welcomed the planned September 2026 launch of direct shipping between the ports of Chattogram and Ranong, which is intended to reduce transit time from 15 days to four days and lower freight costs."
    },
    {
      "text": "During the consultations, Bangladesh requested technical assistance from Thailand to convert agricultural waste into renewable electricity to support a national goal of achieving 20 percent renewable energy generation by 2030. On regional matters, Bangladesh acknowledged Thailand’s support for its candidacy as an ASEAN Sectoral Dialogue Partner and its accession to the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership (RCEP). Foreign Secretary Siam also sought support from Bangkok for the repatriation of displaced Rohingyas to Myanmar. To combat cross-border crimes such as narcotics trade, arms smuggling, human trafficking, online scams, and the trade of drugs, both countries agreed to work toward establishing a joint committee under their respective home ministries. Looking toward the 55th anniversary of diplomatic relations next year, both delegations emphasized political exchanges and expressed hope for visits by their respective heads of government to mark the milestone."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Bangladesh to seek extradition of Sheikh Hasina and Asaduzzaman Khan Kamal during talks with India",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Bangladesh intends to press India for the extradition of ousted prime minister Sheikh Hasina, former home minister Asaduzzaman Khan Kamal, and the alleged killers of Sharif Osman Bin Hadi during the fifth Consular Dialogue in Dhaka, according to diplomatic sources in Dhaka and New Delhi told The Daily Star. This would mark the first time Dhaka has raised the issue at such a formal meeting since the July 2024 uprising. Hasina has remained in India since fleeing Bangladesh during the mass uprising in August 2024. Both Hasina and Kamal were sentenced to death in absentia by the International Crimes Tribunal for crimes against humanity in November 2025. Bangladesh previously sought their extradition via a diplomatic note verbale in December 2024 and renewed the request following those death sentences. An anonymous foreign ministry official stated that Dhaka will seek the extradition of these political figures and the alleged killers during the dialogue. India proposed the November 17-18 timeframe for the director-general-level dialogue, which Dhaka accepted; Bangladesh had initially proposed late October, but India preferred November due to Durga Puja festivities."
    }
  ]
}
